DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ttccppbbeenncchh is a small tool that performs throughput benchmarking and con‐
     current sampling of kernel network variables.

     ttccppbbeenncchh is run as a client/server pair.  The server must be invoked with
     the --ss flag, which will cause it to listen for incoming connections.  The
     client must be invoked with the _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e of a listening server to connect
     to.

     Once connected, the client will send TCP or UDP traffic as fast as possi‐
     ble to the server.  Both the client and server will periodically compute
     and display throughput statistics.  The server starts computing these for
     UDP on receipt of the first datagram, and stops for TCP when it has no
     connections.  This display also includes any kernel variables the user
     has selected to sample (using the --kk option, which is only available in
     TCP mode).  A list of available kernel variables may be obtained using
     the --ll option.

     A summary over the periodic throughput statistics is displayed on exit.
     Its accuracy may be increased by decreasing the _i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l.  The summary
     bytes and duration cover the interval from transfer start to process
     exit.  The summary information can also be displayed while ttccppbbeenncchh is
     running by sending it a SIGINFO signal (see the ssttaattuuss argument of
     stty(1) for more information).

     The options are as follows:

     --44      Force ttccppbbeenncchh to use IPv4 addresses only.

     --66      Force ttccppbbeenncchh to use IPv6 addresses only.

     --BB _b_u_f  Specify the size of the internal read/write buffer used by
             ttccppbbeenncchh.  The default is 262144 bytes for TCP client/server and
             UDP server.  In UDP client mode this may be used to specify the
             packet size on the test stream.

     --bb _s_o_u_r_c_e_a_d_d_r
             Specify the IP address to send the packets from, which is useful
             on machines with multiple interfaces.

     --CC _c_e_r_t_f_i_l_e
             Load the public key part of the TLS peer certificate from
             _c_e_r_t_f_i_l_e, in PEM format.  Requires --ss and --cc.

     --cc      Use TLS to connect or listen.

     --DD      Enable debugging on the socket.

     --KK _k_e_y_f_i_l_e
             Load the TLS private key from _k_e_y_f_i_l_e, in PEM format.  Requires
             --ss and --cc.

     --kk _k_v_a_r_s
             Specify one or more kernel variables to monitor; multiple vari‐
             ables must be separated with commas.  This option is only valid
             in TCP mode.  The default is not to monitor any variables.

     --ll      List the name of kernel variables available for monitoring and
             exit.

     --nn _c_o_n_n_e_c_t_i_o_n_s
             Use the given number of TCP connections (default: 1).  UDP is
             connectionless so this option isn't valid.

     --pp _p_o_r_t
             Specify the port used for the test stream (default: 12345).

     --RR      In client mode the write buffer size is randomized up to the size
             specified via --BB.

     --rr _i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l
             Specify the statistics interval reporting rate in milliseconds
             (default: 1000).  If set to 0, nothing is printed.

     --SS _s_p_a_c_e
             Set the size of the socket buffer used for the test stream.  On
             the client this option will resize the send buffer; on the server
             it will resize the receive buffer.

     --ss      Place ttccppbbeenncchh in server mode, where it will listen on all inter‐
             faces for incoming connections.  It defaults to using TCP if --uu
             is not specified.

     --TT _k_e_y_w_o_r_d
             Change the IPv4 TOS or IPv6 TCLASS value.  _k_e_y_w_o_r_d may be one of
             _c_r_i_t_i_c_a_l, _i_n_e_t_c_o_n_t_r_o_l, _l_o_w_d_e_l_a_y, _n_e_t_c_o_n_t_r_o_l, _t_h_r_o_u_g_h_p_u_t,
             _r_e_l_i_a_b_i_l_i_t_y, or one of the DiffServ Code Points: _e_f, _a_f_1_1 _._._.
             _a_f_4_3, _c_s_0 _._._. _c_s_7; or a number in either hex or decimal.

             For TLS options, _k_e_y_w_o_r_d specifies a value in the form of a
             _k_e_y=_v_a_l_u_e pair: cciipphheerrss, which allows the supported TLS ciphers
             to be specified (see tls_config_set_ciphers(3) for further de‐
             tails) or pprroottooccoollss, which allows the supported TLS protocols to
             be specified (see tls_config_parse_protocols(3) for further de‐
             tails).  Specifying TLS options requires --cc.

     --tt _s_e_c_s
             Stop after _s_e_c_s seconds.

     --UU      Use AF_UNIX sockets instead of IPv4 or IPv6 sockets.  In client
             and server mode _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e is used as the path to the AF_UNIX
             socket.

     --uu      Use UDP instead of TCP; this must be specified on both the client
             and the server.  Transmitted packets per second (TX PPS) will be
             accounted on the client side, while received packets per second
             (RX PPS) will be accounted on the server side.  UDP has no Proto‐
             col Control Block (PCB) so the --kk flags don't apply.

     --VV _r_t_a_b_l_e
             Set the routing table to be used.

     --vv      Display verbose output.  If specified more than once, increase
             the detail of information displayed.

